const t = (title, theory, example, question, correct, distractors, reason) => ({title,theory,example,quiz:{question,correct,distractors,reason}});
const s = (id,name,topics,matches) => ({id,name,topics,matches});

export const curriculum = [
s('network','Network Theory',[
t('Voltage, current, charge, power, energy and Ohm’s law','Voltage is energy per unit charge. Current is the rate at which charge passes a point, i = dq/dt. A linear resistor obeys v = iR. With current entering the positive-voltage terminal, p = vi is power absorbed and electrical energy is the integral of power over time.','A 6 Ω resistor with 2 A through it drops 12 V and absorbs 24 W. If held for 5 s it absorbs 120 J.','A 4 Ω resistor carries 3 A. What power does it absorb?','36 W',['12 W','9 W','48 W'],'Use i²R, which gives 3² × 4 = 36 W.'),
t('Elements, sources and source transformation','A passive element dissipates or stores energy. An active element can supply net energy. An independent source sets its value directly, while a dependent source follows a circuit voltage or current. An ideal voltage source has zero output resistance and an ideal current source has infinite output resistance. Practical models add series resistance to the former and parallel resistance to the latter.','A 12 V source in series with 4 Ω has the same terminal behavior as a 3 A source in parallel with 4 Ω. Preserve current direction relative to voltage polarity.','What current source is equivalent to 15 V in series with 5 Ω?','3 A in parallel with 5 Ω',['3 A in series with 5 Ω','75 A in parallel with 5 Ω','5 A in parallel with 3 Ω'],'Divide the source voltage by the series resistance and place the same resistance in parallel.'),
t('Series, parallel, voltage divider, current divider and star–delta','Series elements share current; parallel elements share voltage. In a resistive voltage divider, a resistor gets the fraction R/ΣR of the applied voltage. In parallel, current divides in proportion to conductance. A delta converts to a star by taking the product of the two delta arms touching each terminal and dividing by the sum of all three arms. For a star-to-delta conversion, divide the sum of pairwise star products by the opposite star arm.','Three equal 6 Ω delta arms give three 2 Ω star arms. Three equal 2 Ω star arms convert back to three 6 Ω delta arms.','A delta has three 9 Ω arms. What is each equivalent star resistance?','3 Ω',['9 Ω','27 Ω','6 Ω'],'Each star arm is 9 × 9 divided by 27.'),
t('Nodes, branches, loops, meshes, KCL and KVL','A node is an equipotential connection region. A branch joins two nodes through a circuit path. A loop is any closed path; a mesh is a loop that contains no smaller loop in a planar drawing. KCL balances currents entering and leaving a node. KVL makes the signed voltage changes around a loop sum to zero in the standard lumped model.','If 8 mA enters a node and 3 mA leaves through one branch, the other outgoing branch carries 5 mA.','Which law follows from conservation of charge at a node?','KCL',['KVL','Maximum power transfer','Reciprocity'],'KCL balances the current flow into and out of the node.'),
t('Nodal and mesh analysis, supernodes and supermeshes','Nodal analysis chooses a reference and writes KCL using branch currents expressed by node voltages. A voltage source between two nonreference nodes forms a supernode, so write KCL around both nodes and add the source voltage constraint. Mesh analysis uses loop currents and KVL. A current source shared by two meshes creates a supermesh and a separate current constraint.','A 5 V source with plus at node A and minus at node B gives VA − VB = 5 V in addition to supernode KCL.','An ideal current source lies on the common branch of two meshes. What should you use?','A supermesh and a current constraint',['A supernode and a voltage constraint','Two independent KVL equations through the source','A voltage divider'],'Write KVL around the outer perimeter and use the current source to relate the two mesh currents.'),
t('Superposition, Thévenin, Norton, matching and reciprocity','For linear circuits, calculate voltage or current contributions from one independent source at a time and add them. Keep dependent sources active. A Thévenin model uses open-circuit voltage and series impedance. A Norton model uses short-circuit current and parallel impedance. Maximum average AC power requires a conjugate load if it is freely adjustable. Reciprocity means the excitation and response ports can be interchanged under the specified reciprocal linear-network conditions.','If Vth = 10 V and Rth = 5 Ω, the Norton current is 2 A. Maximum DC load power occurs at a 5 Ω load.','When using superposition, how do you turn off an independent ideal voltage source?','Replace it by a short circuit',['Replace it by an open circuit','Keep its original voltage','Replace it by a current source'],'Setting an ideal voltage source to 0 V enforces zero voltage across its terminals.'),
t('Capacitors, inductors, RC, RL and RLC responses','Capacitor voltage and inductor current are continuous at switching unless an impulse is applied. Calculate the initial state from the old circuit and the final state from the new circuit. For first-order switching, the deviation from the final value falls as exp(−t/τ), with τ = RthC or L/Rth. In RLC circuits the characteristic roots set underdamped, critically damped or overdamped natural behavior. The forced response comes from the input.','A 10 V step into an initially uncharged RC reaches 6.32 V at one time constant. The stored voltage then approaches 10 V.','Which quantity normally cannot jump instantly in an ideal inductor?','Inductor current',['Inductor voltage','Resistor current','Source voltage'],'A finite voltage produces a finite current slope, di/dt = v/L.'),
t('AC phasors, impedance, admittance, RMS and power','Represent sinusoids at one frequency as complex phasors. Resistor, inductor and capacitor impedances are R, jωL and 1/(jωC), and admittance is the reciprocal of impedance. For RMS phasors, complex power is VI*. Its real part is watts, its imaginary part is vars and its magnitude is volt-amperes. Power factor is the cosine of the voltage-current phase angle for sinusoidal waveforms.','A 10 V RMS source and 2 A RMS current at 60° lag give apparent power 20 VA and real power 10 W.','A sinusoid has a peak voltage of 10 V. What is its RMS voltage?','About 7.07 V',['5 V','10 V','About 14.14 V'],'Divide the peak by √2 for a sine wave.'),
t('Series and parallel resonance, Q and half-power bandwidth','Reactances cancel in an ideal series RLC at ω₀ = 1/√LC, giving minimum impedance and maximum current. An ideal parallel RLC with a shunt resistor has maximum input impedance at resonance. At the half-power frequencies the response power is half its resonant value, or the corresponding response amplitude is 1/√2 of peak. Q = f₀/BW for the standard RLC half-power response.','If f₀ = 100 kHz and the two half-power frequencies are separated by 5 kHz, Q = 20.','At series resonance, what is true of the input impedance of a series RLC?','It is real and minimum',['It is infinite','It equals jωL','It is purely capacitive'],'The L and C reactances cancel, leaving only the series resistance.'),
t('Mutual inductance, dots and two-port parameters','A changing current in one coil induces voltage in the other. Mutual terms have the same sign when reference currents enter both dotted terminals and voltages are referenced positive at the dots. Z parameters relate voltages to currents; Y parameters reverse that relation. h parameters pair input voltage and output current with input current and output voltage. ABCD parameters describe a cascade using output current directed out of port 2.','Find Z11 = V1/I1 while I2 = 0, which means the output port is open. ABCD matrices multiply in cascade order.','How is Z11 measured?','With port 2 open',['With port 2 shorted','With port 1 open','With equal port voltages'],'Z parameters use currents as independent variables, so set I2 to zero.')
],[['KCL','Charge balance at a node'],['Supermesh','Shared current source between two meshes'],['Norton model','Current source with parallel impedance'],['ABCD parameters','Matrix multiplication for cascaded two-ports']]),

s('devices','Electronic Devices',[
t('Energy bands and material classes','Electrons occupy allowed energy bands separated by forbidden gaps. The valence band is the highest normally filled band at low temperature and the conduction band accommodates mobile electrons. Conductors have partially filled or overlapping bands. Semiconductors have a gap small enough for thermal excitation to matter, while insulators typically have a much larger gap.','Silicon’s gap is about 1.12 eV near room temperature. Temperature or light can excite carriers across the gap.','What does band gap mean?','Energy difference between valence-band top and conduction-band bottom',['Average energy of all electrons','Voltage across the sample','Distance between two atoms'],'The gap is an energy interval with no allowed electron states in the ideal crystal.'),
t('Silicon, germanium, intrinsic and doped materials','Intrinsic material has equal electron and hole concentrations. Germanium has a smaller band gap than silicon and therefore a larger intrinsic carrier concentration near room temperature. Donor impurities create n-type material, with electrons as majority carriers. Acceptors create p-type material, with holes as majority carriers. Doping does not ordinarily create a net bulk charge.','Phosphorus is a donor in silicon. Boron is an acceptor. Both affect conductivity by changing carrier concentrations.','Which material has the larger intrinsic carrier concentration at room temperature?','Germanium',['Silicon','Both are identical','It is zero for both'],'Germanium’s smaller band gap makes thermal carrier generation more likely.'),
t('Carriers, Fermi level, mobility, drift and diffusion','Holes represent missing valence electrons. In equilibrium the Fermi level describes occupancy and shifts toward the conduction band in n-type material and toward the valence band in p-type material. Drift results from electric field, with low-field speed magnitude μE. Diffusion results from a concentration gradient. Generation produces electron–hole pairs, and recombination removes them.','In n-type silicon, electrons dominate conduction, but holes still exist as minority carriers. Illumination can create additional pairs.','What drives diffusion?','A carrier concentration gradient',['Only an electric field','Only an applied magnetic field','A constant carrier concentration'],'Random motion produces net movement from high concentration to low concentration.'),
t('PN junction, depletion region, bias and diode equation','At a PN junction, diffusion leaves ionized dopants in a depletion region. The resulting field establishes a built-in potential. Forward bias lowers the barrier and drives exponential current. Reverse bias widens the depletion region and usually gives a small reverse current before breakdown. The ideal relation is ID = IS[exp(VD/(nVT))−1].','At 300 K, VT ≈ 25.9 mV. For n = 1 the forward current multiplies by roughly ten for each additional 60 mV well above the saturation-current floor.','What does forward bias do to the junction barrier?','Reduces it',['Increases it','Leaves it unchanged','Reverses the doping types'],'Forward bias opposes the built-in junction field and lets more carriers cross.'),
t('Leakage, breakdown and diode capacitances','Reverse leakage exists before breakdown and generally increases with temperature. Zener breakdown is tunneling through a narrow depletion region. Avalanche breakdown comes from impact ionization. Junction capacitance reflects depletion charge and usually decreases as reverse bias rises. Diffusion capacitance reflects injected charge and can dominate during forward conduction.','A reverse-biased junction widens as reverse voltage rises, so its depletion capacitance drops.','What dominates avalanche breakdown?','Impact ionization',['Quantum tunneling alone','Ohmic conduction','Radiative recombination'],'Carriers gain energy and generate additional electron–hole pairs by collisions.'),
t('Zener, LED, photodiode, solar cell, Schottky and varactor','A Zener provides a reference in reverse breakdown with current limiting. An LED emits light under forward bias. A photodiode detects light, often under reverse bias, while a solar cell supplies power from light. A Schottky uses a metal–semiconductor junction and often has low forward drop and fast recovery. A varactor uses reverse-bias-dependent capacitance.','In a voltage-controlled oscillator, a varactor changes the resonant capacitance as its reverse voltage varies.','Which diode provides voltage-controlled capacitance?','Varactor',['LED','Schottky rectifier','Solar cell'],'The varactor’s reverse bias changes depletion width and therefore capacitance.'),
t('BJTs, operating regions, α/β and CB/CE/CC configurations','A BJT contains emitter, base and collector. In forward active, the emitter junction is forward biased and the collector junction reverse biased. In cutoff, current is small, and in saturation both junctions conduct forward. α = IC/IE and β = IC/IB, with β = α/(1−α). CE typically inverts voltage, CB has low input resistance, and CC is a noninverting emitter follower.','If α = 0.99, then β = 0.99/0.01 = 99.','In forward active operation, β = 100 and IB = 20 µA. What is IC?','2 mA',['20 mA','0.2 mA','100 µA'],'Multiply the base current by the current gain β.'),
t('JFET, MOS capacitor and MOSFET operating regions','A JFET uses a reverse-biased gate junction to control a normally conducting channel. Drain-end pinch-off begins the current-saturation region; gate cutoff is a separate condition. A MOS capacitor exhibits accumulation, depletion and inversion. Enhancement MOSFETs need channel-forming gate bias, while depletion types conduct at zero gate bias. For long-channel NMOS, saturation starts when VDS ≥ VGS−VT above threshold. For PMOS, use corresponding source-referenced polarities.','For NMOS with VGS = 2 V and VT = 0.8 V, VDS = 0.5 V gives triode operation and VDS = 1.5 V gives saturation.','Which condition marks the start of long-channel NMOS saturation above threshold?','VDS ≥ VGS − VT',['VGS = 0','VDS < VGS − VT','VDS must equal VT'],'Compare drain-source voltage to the gate overdrive VGS−VT.')
],[['Varactor','Voltage-dependent junction capacitance'],['Drift','Motion driven by electric field'],['Avalanche','Carrier multiplication by impact ionization'],['Common collector','Emitter follower']]),

s('analog','Analog Electronics',[
t('Half-wave, centre-tapped and bridge rectifiers','A rectifier converts alternating current into one-polarity pulses. A half-wave rectifier uses one half-cycle. A centre-tapped full-wave rectifier uses alternate transformer halves, and a bridge uses two conducting diodes per half-cycle. Both full-wave topologies produce ripple at twice the input frequency.','With 50 Hz input, an unfiltered bridge produces 100 Hz output pulses.','What is the ripple frequency of a 60 Hz full-wave rectifier?','120 Hz',['30 Hz','60 Hz','240 Hz'],'Each input cycle produces two output pulses.'),
t('Efficiency, ripple, PIV, capacitor filters and Zener regulation','Rectification efficiency is DC load power divided by AC input power. Ripple factor is RMS AC ripple divided by DC output. PIV is the largest reverse voltage a diode must withstand. A reservoir capacitor charges near the peaks and discharges into the load between peaks, with ripple roughly IL/(frC). A series resistor limits current into a Zener shunt regulator.','For IL = 10 mA, ripple frequency 100 Hz and C = 1000 µF, the approximate peak-to-peak ripple is 0.1 V.','What does doubling the reservoir capacitance do to ripple at the same load?','Approximately halves it',['Approximately doubles it','Leaves it unchanged','Doubles ripple frequency'],'The capacitor loses less voltage during the same time between peaks.'),
t('Clippers, clampers and voltage multipliers','Clippers limit a waveform above or below a threshold. Clampers shift its DC level while ideally preserving peak-to-peak swing. Voltage multipliers use diode–capacitor stages to stack voltages. Their output sags under load and depends on capacitance, frequency and diode drops.','A clamper can shift a ±5 V sine wave to approximately 0–10 V in the ideal case.','Which circuit ideally shifts the DC level while preserving peak-to-peak voltage?','Clamper',['Clipper','Comparator','Bridge rectifier'],'A clamp establishes a voltage reference for one waveform extreme.'),
t('Transistor bias, DC load line, Q-point and thermal stability','Biasing establishes DC terminal values before an AC signal arrives. A load line describes the voltage–current combinations allowed by the external circuit. Its intersection with the device characteristic is the Q-point. Emitter or source degeneration adds local negative feedback that reduces sensitivity to device and temperature changes.','For a CE stage with emitter at ground, VCE = VCC − ICRC. Choose the Q-point so the input signal can swing without forcing cutoff or saturation.','What is the Q-point?','The no-signal DC operating point',['The frequency of maximum gain','The maximum reverse voltage','The input at full cutoff only'],'It fixes the operating current and voltages around which small-signal behavior is calculated.'),
t('Small-signal CE/CB/CC and CS/CG/CD amplifiers','Linearize the device around its bias point. Transconductance describes small output-current change per input-voltage change. CE and CS usually invert and offer voltage gain. CB and CG are noninverting and have low input resistance at the emitter or source. CC and CD are followers with voltage gain below one and relatively low output resistance. Load resistance changes the gain.','A CS stage with gm = 2 mS and effective drain load 5 kΩ has gain approximately −10 when the source is AC-grounded.','Which MOS configuration is a source follower?','Common drain',['Common source','Common gate','Common body'],'The gate drives the device and the output is taken from the source.'),
t('Frequency response, coupling, bypass and multistage gain','Coupling capacitors block DC between stages and establish low-frequency poles. Bypass capacitors reduce AC degeneration when their impedance is small. Device and stray capacitances limit high-frequency gain, and the Miller effect can enlarge effective input capacitance in an inverting stage. Cascaded stage gains multiply when loading has been included.','Two stages each with gain magnitude 10 give overall gain magnitude 100, or 40 dB, if the given gains already account for loading.','Which effect can increase effective input capacitance in an inverting amplifier?','Miller effect',['Avalanche multiplication','Source transformation','Nyquist sampling'],'A capacitance across an inverting stage sees a large voltage change across its terminals.'),
t('Differential amplifiers, current mirrors and CMRR','A differential amplifier amplifies the difference between two inputs while suppressing signals shared by both. A current mirror uses matched devices and a reference branch to copy current, subject to compliance voltage and mismatch. CMRR is the magnitude of differential gain divided by common-mode gain, commonly in dB using 20 log₁₀.','If differential gain is 100 and common-mode gain is 0.1, CMRR = 1000 or 60 dB.','What should an ideal differential amplifier do to equal signals on both inputs?','Reject them',['Amplify them by twice the differential gain','Convert them to a ramp','Always saturate'],'Equal inputs create zero differential voltage.'),
t('Ideal and practical op-amps, virtual ground and basic stages','An ideal op-amp draws no input current. With negative feedback and a linear output, its input voltages nearly match. Inverting gain is −Rf/Rin. Noninverting gain is 1+Rf/Rg, and a follower gives unity gain. Real op-amps have offset, finite gain-bandwidth, slew rate, input limits and output swing limits.','With Rf = 10 kΩ and Rin = 2 kΩ, 0.2 V in gives −1 V ideally.','What is the gain of an ideal voltage follower?','+1',['−1','0','Infinite'],'Feedback connects the output directly to the inverting input, so the output follows the noninverting input.'),
t('Summer, difference amp, integrator, differentiator and comparators','A summing amplifier adds weighted inputs, while a difference amplifier subtracts them using matched ratios. An inverting integrator uses capacitive feedback and a differentiator uses a capacitive input. Comparators switch high or low depending on input ordering. A Schmitt trigger uses positive feedback to produce distinct rising and falling thresholds.','An ideal inverting integrator driven by a constant positive voltage produces a downward ramp until it hits a supply limit.','Which circuit has two switching thresholds for noise immunity?','Schmitt trigger',['Voltage follower','Ideal integrator','Inverting summer'],'Positive feedback introduces hysteresis.'),
t('Negative and positive feedback, gain-bandwidth and Barkhausen','Negative feedback generally reduces gain sensitivity and distortion and can extend bandwidth in a single-pole amplifier. Positive feedback can cause switching or sustain oscillations. The Barkhausen steady-state condition at the oscillation frequency is unity loop magnitude and total phase 0° modulo 360°. Startup usually needs small-signal loop gain above unity.','An oscillator may start with gain above one and then settle to effective loop gain of one as its amplitude grows.','At steady oscillation, what is the loop-gain magnitude in the Barkhausen condition?','1',['0','0.5','Infinite'],'After each cycle the feedback recreates the same amplitude and phase.'),
t('RC, Wien bridge, Hartley, Colpitts and crystal oscillators','RC phase-shift oscillators obtain phase through an RC network. A Wien bridge selects a frequency using a lead–lag RC network, with f₀ = 1/(2πRC) for equal values. Hartley uses an inductive divider and Colpitts a capacitive divider. A quartz crystal uses mechanical resonance for a high-Q, stable frequency reference.','An equal-RC Wien network with R = 10 kΩ and C = 10 nF selects about 1.59 kHz.','Which oscillator uses a capacitive voltage divider?','Colpitts',['Hartley','Wien bridge only','RC phase-shift'],'The feedback divider in a Colpitts tank consists of two capacitors.'),
t('Power amplifier classes, active filters and 555 timer','Class A conducts for 360°, class B for 180°, class AB for more than 180° but less than 360°, and class C for less than 180°. Push-pull class B can suffer crossover distortion near zero; AB bias reduces it. Active low-pass, high-pass, band-pass and band-stop filters select the appropriate frequency regions. A 555 is monostable for one timed pulse and astable for a repeating waveform.','A 555 monostable timing pulse is approximately 1.1RC in the standard circuit.','Which 555 mode repeatedly oscillates without an external trigger?','Astable',['Monostable','Comparator-only','One-shot'],'The timing capacitor charges and discharges repeatedly between the internal thresholds.')
],[['CMRR','Differential gain divided by common-mode gain'],['Clamper','Moves the waveform DC level'],['Hartley','Inductive feedback divider'],['Schmitt trigger','Uses hysteresis']]),

s('digital','Digital Electronics',[
t('Binary, octal, decimal and hexadecimal','Each digit has a place value equal to a power of its base. Convert to decimal by summing digit × place value. Convert integers from decimal by repeated division and reading remainders in reverse. Group binary into sets of three for octal or four for hexadecimal.','101101₂ = 45₁₀ = 55₈ = 2D₁₆.','What is 1101₂ in decimal?','13',['11','15','19'],'The place values contribute 8+4+0+1.'),
t('Binary arithmetic, signed numbers, complements and overflow','One’s complement flips all bits, and two’s complement flips them and adds one. The n-bit two’s-complement range is −2^(n−1) to 2^(n−1)−1. Signed overflow occurs when two same-sign operands yield the opposite sign. Carry out alone does not tell you whether signed overflow occurred.','In four bits, +5 is 0101 and −5 is 1011. Adding +5 to +4 yields 1001, indicating signed overflow.','What range can four-bit two’s complement represent?','−8 to +7',['−7 to +8','−8 to +8','0 to 15'],'One bit pattern represents zero, and the negative side has one additional value.'),
t('BCD, Gray code and parity','BCD represents each decimal digit separately with four bits. Adjacent values in reflected Gray code differ in one bit, limiting transition ambiguity. A parity bit makes the total number of ones even or odd. It detects an odd number of flips and does not locate the faulty bit.','Decimal 29 in BCD is 0010 1001. For even parity, 1011 needs a parity bit of 1.','Which code changes one bit between neighboring count values?','Gray code',['BCD','Ordinary binary in every transition','ASCII'],'Adjacent Gray code words are designed to differ in exactly one position.'),
t('Logic gates, truth tables and universal gates','Truth tables list each input combination and output. AND requires all inputs high, OR requires any high, XOR detects inequality for two inputs, and NOT inverts. NAND and NOR are universal because each alone can implement NOT, AND and OR.','Tie both inputs of a NAND together to get NOT. Follow a NAND by such an inverter to get AND.','Which gate alone can implement any Boolean function?','NAND',['XOR','AND','OR'],'NAND can implement the basic gate set, including inversion.'),
t('Boolean algebra, SOP/POS, minterms, maxterms and K-maps','De Morgan’s laws exchange AND and OR when inversion is pushed through them. A minterm is true at one input combination, while a maxterm is false at one. SOP sums products of literals and POS multiplies sums. A K-map groups adjacent 1s for SOP or adjacent 0s for POS, using powers of two and optional don’t-cares.','AB + A¬B reduces to A because B + ¬B = 1. In a K-map these minterms form a pair.','What is the SOP grouping size allowed in a K-map?','A power of two',['Any odd integer','Only three cells','Only one cell'],'Each pairwise grouping removes one changing variable, so valid group sizes are 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on.'),
t('Adders, subtractors, comparators, multiplexers and decoders','Half-adders combine two bits and full adders also accept carry-in. A full subtractor accepts borrow-in. Comparators test equality or order. A multiplexer selects one input for a common output; a demultiplexer routes one input to a selected output. A decoder activates an output from an input code, while a priority encoder encodes the highest-priority asserted input.','A 4-to-1 multiplexer needs two select inputs. A 3-to-8 decoder can select one output from a three-bit code.','How many select bits does an 8-to-1 multiplexer need?','3',['2','4','8'],'Three bits represent eight possible channel numbers.'),
t('Latches, SR, JK, D and T flip-flops','A latch is level-sensitive and a flip-flop is usually edge-triggered. SR implements set and reset, JK toggles when J=K=1, D copies its input at the active edge, and T toggles when T=1. Characteristic tables map present state and inputs to the next state. Excitation tables work backward. A level-clocked JK can race around if the active pulse allows repeated toggling.','A D flip-flop sampling D=1 at its active edge stores 1 until a later edge changes it.','What does a JK flip-flop do at its active edge when J=K=1?','Toggles',['Holds','Always sets','Always resets'],'Both asserted inputs select next state ¬Q.'),
t('Synchronous, ripple, mod-N, ring and Johnson counters','Ripple counters clock later stages from earlier outputs, allowing propagation glitches. Synchronous counters clock all stages together. A mod-N counter needs at least ⌈log₂N⌉ flip-flops in binary encoding. A properly initialized n-stage ring counter circulates one active bit through n states. A Johnson counter uses inverted feedback to produce 2n states.','A four-stage Johnson counter has eight valid states in its intended sequence.','How many states does an initialized five-stage Johnson counter cycle through?','10',['5','16','32'],'Inverted feedback fills the register with ones and then empties it, giving 2n states.'),
t('Shift registers, serial/parallel conversion and state machines','Shift registers move stored bits on each clock. Serial-in parallel-out gathers a stream into a word, and parallel-in serial-out transmits a word serially. Finite-state machines specify states and transitions. Moore outputs depend on state alone; Mealy outputs depend on state and current input.','A four-stage serial-in register can collect four incoming bits over four shift clocks.','Which FSM model can change output when the input changes before a state transition?','Mealy',['Moore only','Both models require a state change','Neither model has inputs'],'Mealy outputs are functions of both present state and current input.'),
t('Timing, logic families, fan-out and noise margin','Setup is the required stable interval before the clock edge. Hold is the required interval afterward. Propagation delay is the time from an input event to the corresponding output change. CMOS and TTL differ in levels, drive and power. Fan-out counts supported loads. High noise margin is VOH,min − VIH,min, with a corresponding low-level margin.','If minimum high output is 3.3 V and minimum high input is 2.0 V, the high noise margin is 1.3 V.','Which interval requires data to remain stable after the sampling edge?','Hold time',['Setup time','Clock period','Rise time'],'Hold specifies the minimum required stability immediately after the clock edge.'),
t('ROM, RAM, SRAM, DRAM and programmable logic','ROM retains programmed contents without power. Ordinary RAM is readable and writable and typically volatile. SRAM uses bistable storage and needs no refresh while powered. DRAM stores charge and needs refresh. Programmable logic implements functions using configurable resources such as LUTs, logic blocks and routing.','SRAM commonly serves caches, while denser DRAM serves main memory. FPGA lookup tables implement combinational logic.','Which volatile memory typically requires periodic refresh?','DRAM',['SRAM','ROM','Combinational LUT output'],'Charge stored in DRAM cells leaks over time and must be restored.'),
t('ADC, DAC, quantization, flash, SAR and R–2R ladders','An ADC maps analog samples into codes while a DAC maps codes to analog levels. An ideal n-bit converter has 2ⁿ quantization levels, with ideal step approximately full-scale span/2ⁿ. Flash ADCs use parallel comparisons, while SAR ADCs try bits from most to least significant. R–2R ladders use two resistor values for binary weighting.','An 8-bit ADC spanning 2.56 V has a nominal 10 mV step.','How many comparators does a conventional 3-bit flash ADC need?','7',['3','8','4'],'A flash ADC uses one threshold between adjacent levels, giving 2³−1 comparators.')
],[['NAND','Universal gate'],['Johnson counter','Inverted shift-register feedback'],['Mealy model','Output depends on input and state'],['SAR ADC','Trials proceed from most to least significant bit']]),

s('signals','Signals and Systems',[
t('Signal types, periodicity, symmetry, energy and power','Continuous-time signals use a continuous argument, while discrete-time signals use integer indices. A periodic signal repeats after a nonzero period. A discrete sinusoid is periodic if its normalized frequency in cycles/sample is rational. Even signals satisfy x(−t)=x(t), and odd signals satisfy x(−t)=−x(t). Energy integrates squared magnitude; average power divides finite-window energy by the window length before taking the limit.','A nonzero periodic sine has infinite total energy and finite average power A²/2.','A unit-amplitude sine exists for all time. What type of signal is it?','A power signal',['A finite-energy signal','A zero-power signal','An aperiodic signal'],'It has average power 1/2 and infinite total energy.'),
t('Impulse, step, ramp, exponential and sinusoid','A unit step turns on at the origin. A unit ramp is t times the step. An impulse has unit area and selects the signal value at its location under integration. Exponentials describe growth or decay. A sinusoid is specified by amplitude, frequency and phase.','Integrating a unit impulse gives a step. Differentiating a ramp gives the step in the generalized-signal sense.','What does convolution with δ(t−3) do to x(t)?','Delays x(t) by 3',['Advances x(t) by 3','Scales its amplitude by 3','Differentiates it'],'A shifted impulse implements the same time shift under convolution.'),
t('Time shifting, scaling, reversal and signal operations','For x(t−t₀), positive t₀ moves the waveform right. A positive scale a in x(at) compresses time when a>1 and expands it when a<1. A negative scale also reverses time. To place a feature after a combined transformation, solve for the time at which the new argument equals the original feature time.','A feature at t=4 in x(t) appears at t=3 in x(2t−2), since 2t−2=4.','For x(2t), what happens to the duration of a pulse?','It halves',['It doubles','It stays fixed','It becomes zero'],'The input argument reaches each feature in half the original time.'),
t('Linearity, time invariance, causality, memory and BIBO','Linearity requires superposition. A time-invariant system commutes with a time shift. A causal system cannot depend on future input, and a memoryless one uses only the current input. BIBO stability requires every bounded input to produce a bounded output. Test each property independently.','y(t)=t x(t) is linear and memoryless but time-varying. It is also not BIBO stable because x(t)=1 gives y(t)=t.','Is y(t)=x(t+1) causal?','No, it requires a future input value',['Yes because it is linear','Yes because it is time-invariant','Yes because its gain is one'],'At time t it depends on x one unit later than t.'),
t('LTI systems, impulse/step responses and convolution','An LTI system is determined by its impulse response h. For continuous time, y(t) integrates x(τ)h(t−τ) over τ. In discrete time, the integral becomes a sum. The step response is convolution of h with a step. A continuous-time LTI system with an ordinary impulse response is BIBO stable if the integral of |h| is finite.','Convolving finite sequences of lengths 3 and 4 gives output length at most 6 when their supports are consecutive.','Which operation gives the output of an LTI system?','Convolution of input and impulse response',['Pointwise multiplication of both time signals','Division by the impulse response','Differentiation of input in every case'],'An LTI output is a weighted sum of shifted impulse responses.'),
t('Fourier series, Fourier transform and frequency spectra','Fourier series describes periodic signals using harmonics. A Fourier transform describes spectral content of general signals. A time shift adds linear spectral phase and preserves magnitude. Time convolution becomes frequency multiplication. The exact scaling constants depend on whether frequency is in Hz or rad/s.','A cosine at f₀ has two spectral lines at +f₀ and −f₀ in the two-sided spectrum.','How does a pure time delay change Fourier magnitude?','It leaves it unchanged',['It halves it','It shifts it to positive frequency only','It always doubles it'],'The frequency multiplier for a delay has unit magnitude.'),
t('Laplace transform, ROC, poles, zeros and inverse transform','The bilateral Laplace transform extends frequency to complex s. Poles are denominator roots after cancellation and zeros are numerator roots. The region of convergence (ROC) is the set of s where the transform exists and excludes poles. To invert a rational expression, use partial fractions plus the ROC to decide whether each exponential is right- or left-sided.','For e⁻²ᵗu(t), X(s)=1/(s+2) with ROC Re(s)>−2.','Can the same bilateral Laplace expression represent two signals with different ROCs?','Yes',['No','Only if it contains no poles','Only for sampled signals'],'A distinct ROC can correspond to a distinct time support, such as left-sided versus right-sided.'),
t('Z-transform, ROC, inverse and difference equations','The bilateral Z-transform sums x[n]z⁻ⁿ. If the ROC contains the unit circle, evaluating at z=exp(jω) gives the DTFT. Rational transforms with a right-sided sequence have an ROC outside the outermost pole. Difference equations transform into algebraic equations under zero initial conditions, then inverse transformation returns the response.','For aⁿu[n], X(z)=1/(1−az⁻¹) with |z|>|a|.','A causal rational discrete LTI system has a pole at z=1.2. Is it BIBO stable?','No',['Yes','Only if the pole is real','Only if the input is periodic'],'The causal ROC excludes the unit circle in this case.'),
t('Sampling, Nyquist rate, aliasing and reconstruction','Sampling replicates a baseband spectrum at multiples of the sample rate. Avoid overlap by sampling above twice the highest frequency in the ideally bandlimited case. Aliasing folds higher frequencies into the sampled band. Ideal low-pass reconstruction recovers the original under the sampling assumptions, while practical inputs need an anti-alias filter and a transition band.','A 900 Hz sinusoid sampled at 1 kHz aliases to a 100 Hz sinusoid.','What is the Nyquist rate for a baseband signal bandlimited to 3 kHz?','6 ksample/s',['3 ksample/s','1.5 ksample/s','9 ksample/s'],'The theoretical threshold is twice the highest signal frequency.'),
t('Correlation, energy spectra and power spectra','Autocorrelation compares a signal with delayed copies of itself. Cross-correlation compares two different signals and can estimate their relative delay. The energy spectral density of a finite-energy signal is |X(f)|². For a wide-sense stationary process, its power spectral density is the Fourier transform of its autocorrelation under the standard Wiener–Khinchin relation.','A sharp cross-correlation peak can reveal a timing offset between a received signal and a known reference.','What does zero-lag autocorrelation equal for a deterministic energy signal?','Its total energy',['Its bandwidth','Its peak sample only','Its phase delay'],'At zero lag the signal is integrated against its own conjugate.')
],[['ROC','Determines convergence and helps identify time support'],['Autocorrelation','Compares a waveform with delayed versions of itself'],['Time invariance','Output shifts by the same delay as the input'],['BIBO stability','All bounded inputs produce bounded outputs']]),

s('dsp','Digital Signal Processing',[
t('DTFT and discrete-time frequency response','The DTFT sums x[n]exp(−jωn) and is periodic in ω with period 2π. An LTI filter’s frequency response is the DTFT of its impulse response when it exists. Magnitude changes the amplitude of each sinusoidal component and phase determines its phase shift.','A one-sample delay h[n]=δ[n−1] has response exp(−jω), with unit magnitude and linear phase.','What is the period of the DTFT in angular frequency?','2π',['π','1','It is aperiodic'],'Multiplying ω by an offset of 2π leaves exp(−jωn) unchanged for integer n.'),
t('DFT, inverse DFT, bins and frequency spacing','An N-point DFT evaluates samples at frequencies kfs/N. Its inverse reconstructs the finite block from those frequency samples. The bin spacing is fs/N, but distinguishability of nearby tones depends on the observation duration and window. Zero-padding provides denser frequency samples without adding new time-domain observations.','At fs=8 kHz and N=1024, bins are about 7.8125 Hz apart.','At fs=1 kHz and N=200, what is the bin spacing?','5 Hz',['0.2 Hz','50 Hz','200 Hz'],'Divide sample rate by DFT length.'),
t('Linear versus circular convolution and zero-padding','Linear convolution sums overlapping shifted samples without wraparound. The DFT convolution theorem relates multiplication of DFTs to circular convolution, which wraps sample indices modulo N. To compute linear convolution by DFT, zero-pad both sequences to N≥Lx+Lh−1.','Lengths 5 and 7 need an FFT length of at least 11 to avoid wraparound. A length of 16 is convenient for radix-2.','What is the smallest padding length for convolution of sequences of lengths 4 and 6?','9',['6','8','10'],'The linear-convolution output length is 4+6−1.'),
t('Radix-2 FFT and computational cost','An FFT computes the same DFT more efficiently. Radix-2 algorithms recursively split a power-of-two problem into halves, then combine it using butterflies and complex twiddle factors. A direct DFT takes order N² operations, while radix-2 FFT takes order N log₂N.','A 1024-point radix-2 FFT has ten stages because log₂1024=10.','Does an FFT change the DFT values?','No, it is a faster algorithm for the same transform',['Yes, it removes every odd bin','Yes, it computes a different transform','Yes, it always doubles spectral resolution'],'The computational structure changes, while the mathematical output is the DFT.'),
t('FIR and IIR filters, stability, phase and direct forms','An FIR has finite impulse response and any finite set of coefficients gives BIBO stability. Symmetric or antisymmetric FIR coefficients support linear phase. An IIR uses recursion and a causal rational IIR is stable when its actual poles are inside the unit circle. Direct forms implement delays, multipliers and feedback corresponding to the difference equation.','An FIR with coefficients [¼, ½, ¼] is symmetric and has one sample of group delay in its passband.','Where must the poles of a stable causal rational IIR lie?','Strictly inside the unit circle',['Strictly outside the unit circle','Exactly on the unit circle','In the left half-plane only'],'For the causal ROC to include the unit circle, all finite poles must have magnitude below one.'),
t('Windowing, leakage and basic filter design','Taking a finite record multiplies the signal by a window and convolves its ideal spectrum with the window spectrum. Off-bin tones spread into other bins. Tapering usually reduces sidelobes while widening the main lobe. Window-method FIR design truncates and tapers the ideal impulse response, trading transition width against stopband rejection.','A Hann window typically reduces leakage sidelobes compared with a rectangular window, at the cost of a broader main lobe.','Which is a common consequence of a tapered window?','Lower sidelobes and wider main lobe',['Every tone becomes a single bin','Higher sidelobes and always narrower main lobe','The sample rate doubles'],'Tapering makes record ends smoother, reducing sidelobes while sacrificing some resolution.'),
t('Interpolation and decimation','Upsampling by L inserts L−1 zeros between samples, then a low-pass filter suppresses spectral images and reconstructs intermediate samples. Downsampling by M keeps every Mth sample. An anti-alias low-pass filter normally comes before downsampling. Multirate implementations can combine these operations efficiently.','Decimating 48 ksample/s by 3 gives 16 ksample/s. Filter the input so frequencies at or above the new 8 kHz Nyquist limit do not alias.','Where should the anti-alias filter be placed in a decimator?','Before downsampling',['After downsampling only','After the DFT only','It is unnecessary for every input'],'Once spectral components alias during downsampling, a later filter cannot undo overlap.')
],[['FFT','Efficient DFT algorithm'],['Zero-padding for convolution','Prevents cyclic wraparound at adequate length'],['FIR','Finite impulse response'],['Decimation','Reduces the sample rate']]),

s('control','Control Systems',[
t('Open and closed loops, modelling and transfer functions','An open loop acts without observing the output. A closed loop measures output and corrects the error. Model the plant using physical laws such as force balance or KCL. The transfer function is output divided by input in the Laplace domain under zero initial conditions. For negative feedback, T = G/(1+GH).','With G=4/(s+2) and unity feedback, the closed-loop transfer is 4/(s+6).','Which loop measures its output to correct the input?','Closed loop',['Open loop','Feedforward only','Purely disconnected loop'],'Feedback uses measured output to form an error signal.'),
t('Block diagrams, signal-flow graphs and Mason’s formula','Multiply blocks in series and add those in parallel with their signs. Reduce a simple feedback loop using G/(1+GH). In a signal-flow graph, Mason’s gain is the sum of forward path gains weighted by their path cofactors, divided by Δ. Δ subtracts individual loop gains, adds products of pairs of non-touching loops, and continues with alternating signs. A path cofactor excludes loops touching that path.','With one forward path P and a single loop of gain −GH touching it, the gain is P/(1+GH).','Which loop pairs contribute to the second-order product terms of Mason’s Δ?','Pairs of non-touching loops',['Pairs sharing a node','Any two paths','Only loops on the forward path'],'Only loops with no common node count as non-touching.'),
t('Poles, zeros, first- and second-order systems and test inputs','Poles are denominator roots after cancellation and determine natural modes and transfer-function stability. Zeros are numerator roots and shape the response. A first-order pole at −1/τ gives an exponential time constant. A standard second-order denominator is s²+2ζωₙs+ωₙ². Common test inputs are impulse, step and ramp.','For 1/(2s+1), the pole is −0.5 s⁻¹ and the step time constant is 2 s.','What is the pole of a first-order system with time constant 4 s?','−0.25 s⁻¹',['+0.25 s⁻¹','−4 s⁻¹','+4 s⁻¹'],'The pole is −1/τ.'),
t('Time response metrics and damping','Rise time measures how quickly output moves toward its final value, with thresholds specified by convention. Peak time is the first overshoot peak. Overshoot is the excess relative to the final value. Settling time is the time after which the output stays inside a chosen band. For a standard underdamped second-order response, the 2% settling estimate is 4/(ζωₙ) and peak time is π/[ωₙ√(1−ζ²)].','For ζ=0.5 and ωₙ=4 rad/s, the 2% settling estimate is 2 s.','At fixed ωₙ, what usually happens to percent overshoot when ζ rises within 0<ζ<1?','It decreases',['It increases','It stays constant','It equals the rise time'],'Greater damping reduces the peak above the final value.'),
t('Steady-state error, system type and error constants','For unity feedback, system type is the number of uncancelled integrators in the open-loop path. The position, velocity and acceleration constants are limits of G(s), sG(s) and s²G(s) as s approaches zero. Unit step, unit ramp and unit parabolic steady-state errors are 1/(1+Kp), 1/Kv and 1/Ka when the final-value conditions hold.','A stable type-1 unity-feedback system has zero step error and may have a finite ramp error.','Which error constant controls unit-ramp error?','Kv',['Kp','Ka','Gain margin'],'A unit ramp has Laplace transform 1/s², so the final-value limit involves sG(s).'),
t('Routh–Hurwitz, relative stability and root locus','Routh’s array counts right-half-plane roots by first-column sign changes. A zero leading entry or all-zero row requires special treatment. Relative stability asks how far poles lie from the imaginary axis; a shifted-axis test can assess a specified margin. Root locus traces closed-loop poles as loop gain varies, starting at open-loop poles and ending at zeros or infinity.','A Routh first column +, +, −, + has two sign changes and therefore two right-half-plane roots in the regular case.','Where do conventional positive-gain root-locus branches start as gain tends to zero?','Open-loop poles',['Open-loop zeros only','Gain-crossover frequencies','The imaginary axis in every case'],'At zero gain, the closed-loop characteristic roots reduce to the open-loop pole locations.'),
t('Bode plots, margins and Nyquist stability','A Bode plot gives loop magnitude in dB and phase versus frequency. Gain crossover occurs at 0 dB, while phase crossover occurs at −180°. Phase margin measures phase distance to −180° at gain crossover. Gain margin measures gain change needed to reach unity at phase crossover. Nyquist uses encirclements of −1 together with open-loop unstable poles to infer closed-loop stability.','A loop angle of −135° at gain crossover gives a phase margin of 45°.','At which frequency do you read phase margin?','Gain crossover',['Phase crossover','Natural frequency in all cases','DC only'],'Phase margin is computed where the loop magnitude equals one.'),
t('P, PI, PD, PID and lead–lag compensation','P action scales present error. I action accumulates error and increases low-frequency gain. D action responds to rate of change and can add damping but is noise-sensitive. PI, PD and PID combine these actions. A lead compensator adds positive phase over a band, a lag improves low-frequency gain relative to high-frequency gain, and lead–lag combines both effects.','An ideal PI controller has Kp + Ki/s and introduces an integrator. Check the resulting closed-loop stability before using it to improve tracking.','Which compensator is commonly used to add positive phase near crossover?','Lead',['Lag only','Pure integrator','Dead zone'],'A lead network places its zero at a lower break frequency than its pole.'),
t('State equations, controllability and observability','A linear state-space model uses ẋ=Ax+Bu and y=Cx+Du, where states summarize what is needed to predict future behavior. A system is controllable when inputs can move the state between arbitrary values in finite time. It is observable when outputs determine the initial state under known inputs. The corresponding finite-dimensional rank tests use [B,AB,…] and vertically stacked [C;CA;…].','A two-state model needs rank 2 for each test. A disconnected state that cannot affect any measured output is unobservable.','Which matrix starts the controllability test?','B',['C','D','The identity in all systems'],'B describes how inputs directly act on states, and powers of A reveal indirect effects.')
],[['Phase margin','Read at gain crossover'],['Routh sign changes','Count right-half-plane roots'],['Observability','Infer internal state from measured output'],['PI controller','Proportional action plus integration']]),

s('comm','Communication Systems',[
t('Baseband, passband, carriers, spectrum and rates','Baseband signals occupy frequencies near zero. Modulation shifts information to a band around a carrier. Bandwidth is a specified frequency span and depends on the measurement convention. Bit rate is bits/s; symbol rate is symbols/s. For fixed binary labels of an M-ary constellation, raw Rb = Rs log₂M.','16-QAM at 2 Msymbol/s carries 8 Mbit/s before protocol and coding overhead.','How many bits per symbol does 16-QAM carry?','4',['2','8','16'],'There are 16 = 2⁴ distinct symbols.'),
t('Noise, thermal noise, SNR, noise figure and Shannon capacity','Thermal noise over bandwidth B is kTB in the available-noise model. SNR is signal power divided by noise power. Noise figure compares input SNR with output SNR. Shannon capacity for an AWGN channel is B log₂(1+S/N), with linear SNR inside the logarithm.','At B=1 MHz and SNR=3 linear, C=2 Mbit/s. A power ratio of 10 dB equals 10 linear.','Which SNR value goes into Shannon’s logarithm for a 20 dB SNR?','100',['20','2','200'],'Convert with 10 raised to SNRdB/10.'),
t('AM, DSB-SC, SSB, VSB, sidebands and power','Conventional AM transmits a carrier and two sidebands. The modulation index for a normalized single tone is the ratio of reference amplitude change to carrier amplitude, and above one ideal envelope detection distorts. DSB-SC suppresses the carrier, SSB sends one sideband, and VSB sends one sideband plus a vestige of the other. For single-tone conventional AM, total power is Pc(1+m²/2).','A 5 kHz message needs 10 kHz for DSB and ideally about 5 kHz for SSB.','How much bandwidth does ideal SSB need for a 4 kHz low-pass message?','4 kHz',['2 kHz','8 kHz','12 kHz'],'Only one translated sideband remains.'),
t('FM, PM, deviation, Carson’s rule and demodulation','FM makes instantaneous carrier frequency track the message. PM makes phase track the message, so its instantaneous frequency depends on the message derivative. For single-tone FM, β=Δf/fm. Carson’s approximation is BW≈2(Δf+fm,max). A discriminator or PLL can recover FM.','A 75 kHz peak deviation with 15 kHz message bandwidth gives approximately 180 kHz occupied bandwidth by Carson’s rule.','In single-tone FM, Δf=30 kHz and fm=10 kHz. What is β?','3',['0.33','20','40'],'FM modulation index is frequency deviation divided by tone frequency.'),
t('Superheterodyne receiver, mixer, LO, IF and image','A superheterodyne mixes selected RF with a local oscillator to make a fixed intermediate frequency, usually |fRF−fLO|. Fixed IF makes gain and filtering easier. The image is the other RF frequency on the opposite side of the LO that also produces the same IF. Front-end selectivity helps reject it.','For high-side LO injection, fLO=fRF+fIF and the image is fRF+2fIF.','With desired RF 100 MHz and high-side LO 110 MHz, where is the image?','120 MHz',['90 MHz','100 MHz','110 MHz'],'Both 100 MHz and 120 MHz are 10 MHz from the LO.'),
t('PAM, PWM, PPM, PCM, DPCM, delta modulation and companding','PAM varies pulse amplitude, PWM varies width, and PPM varies position. PCM samples, quantizes and encodes each sample. DPCM quantizes prediction error. Delta modulation sends a one-bit indication of change and can suffer slope overload on steep inputs. Companding compresses amplitude before quantization and expands afterward, improving relative treatment of weak signals.','An 8-bit PCM stream at 8 ksample/s needs 64 kbit/s before framing.','What quantity does DPCM primarily quantize?','Prediction error',['Absolute carrier phase','Only the largest input sample','The pulse repetition interval'],'Encoding the difference from a prediction can reduce redundancy.'),
t('Line coding, NRZ, RZ, Manchester and ISI','Line coding maps bits to baseband waveforms. NRZ holds a level throughout the bit, RZ returns to zero within the bit, and Manchester includes a mid-bit transition for clock recovery. Intersymbol interference happens when one symbol pulse contributes to other symbols’ decision samples. Pulse shaping and equalization can reduce it.','A Manchester waveform has a transition in the middle of each bit interval; the transition direction depends on the bit convention.','What is a defining feature of Manchester coding?','A transition at the midpoint of every bit',['No transitions for repeated bits','Two bits per amplitude level only','A forced zero level at the end of all bits'],'That mid-bit transition provides a timing cue.'),
t('ASK, FSK, BPSK, QPSK, QAM, I/Q and BER','ASK changes amplitude and FSK changes frequency. BPSK uses two phases, QPSK four, and QAM uses different in-phase and quadrature amplitudes. A constellation plots symbol points in I/Q space. Distance between points and noise determine decision errors. BER is the fraction or probability of erroneous bits under stated conditions.','QPSK sends two bits per symbol. Increasing constellation order can increase data rate at the same symbol rate, but often needs better SNR for the same BER.','Which modulation changes the transmitted frequency according to the symbol?','FSK',['ASK','BPSK','QAM'],'Frequency shift keying selects among discrete frequency choices.'),
t('Coherent detection, matched filtering and Nyquist zero ISI','Coherent detection uses a synchronized phase/frequency reference. Noncoherent detection avoids explicit carrier phase knowledge for suitable schemes. A matched filter correlates with the expected pulse and maximizes sampling-instant output SNR in AWGN. The Nyquist zero-ISI condition requires the overall impulse response to vanish at nonzero integer symbol offsets.','An ideal sampling pulse with h(0)=1 and h(kT)=0 for all nonzero integer k contributes only its intended symbol at each decision time.','What does a matched filter maximize in AWGN?','Output SNR at the decision sampling instant',['Carrier frequency','Occupied bandwidth','The number of symbol errors'],'A pulse-matched impulse response exploits the known signal shape.'),
t('FDM, TDM, CDMA, OFDM, FFT and cyclic prefix','FDM allocates frequency bands, TDM time slots, and CDMA spreading codes. OFDM uses orthogonal subcarriers spaced by 1/Tu and typically uses an IFFT at transmit and FFT at receive. The cyclic prefix copies the end of the useful symbol to its beginning. With adequate prefix duration and timing, the channel convolution can be handled as circular over the useful block.','With Tu=64 µs, subcarriers are 15.625 kHz apart. A 16 µs prefix consumes 20% of total symbol time.','What part of the OFDM symbol determines subcarrier spacing?','Useful symbol duration',['Cyclic prefix duration alone','Guard interval plus useful duration in every case','Number of pilot symbols alone'],'Orthogonality is measured over the useful FFT interval.'),
t('Parity, CRC, Hamming distance and error correction','Parity detects an odd number of bit errors. CRC treats the bit sequence as a polynomial over GF(2) and transmits a division remainder for error detection. Hamming distance counts positions where codewords differ. A code with minimum distance d can detect up to d−1 errors or correct up to ⌊(d−1)/2⌋ errors under the respective guarantees.','A standard Hamming (7,4) code has minimum distance 3 and can correct one error.','How many errors can a minimum-distance-5 code always correct?','2',['1','4','5'],'Take the floor of (5−1)/2.'),
t('Wireless channels, fading, Doppler, diversity and MIMO','Multipath gives delayed replicas, and delay spread can cause frequency selectivity. Motion changes the observed frequency through Doppler. Rayleigh fading models a diffuse channel without a dominant component, and Rician adds a dominant component. Diversity combines independent observations for reliability. MIMO can steer energy through beamforming or send parallel streams through spatial multiplexing when the channel supports them.','In an indoor link, two paths can cancel at one location. Moving the antenna or combining a second branch can reduce the chance that both observations fade together.','Which MIMO technique sends different data streams in parallel?','Spatial multiplexing',['Receive diversity alone','Cyclic prefixing','Carrier frequency recovery'],'Spatial multiplexing uses separate spatial modes for throughput.')
],[['SSB','Transmits one sideband'],['Matched filter','Maximizes SNR at a decision instant in AWGN'],['Cyclic prefix','Copies the end of an OFDM symbol to its front'],['Rician fading','Diffuse components plus a dominant component']]),

s('em','Electromagnetics, Transmission Lines, and RF',[
t('Electric and magnetic fields, Coulomb, Gauss, Faraday and Maxwell','Coulomb’s law gives an inverse-square electrostatic force between point charges. Gauss’s electric law relates closed-surface electric flux density to enclosed charge. Gauss’s magnetic law says net magnetic flux through a closed surface is zero. Faraday links induced voltage to changing magnetic flux. Ampère–Maxwell links magnetic circulation to conduction and displacement currents.','If the separation between two point charges doubles in a uniform medium, their force magnitude falls to one quarter.','Which law relates induced emf to the time change of magnetic flux?','Faraday’s law',['Coulomb’s law','Gauss’s electric law','Ohm’s law'],'For a fixed loop the induced emf is the negative time derivative of flux linkage.'),
t('Boundary conditions, plane waves, polarization and power flow','At interfaces, tangential E is continuous in the usual absence of a magnetic surface current. Surface electric current can cause a jump in tangential H; free surface charge can cause a jump in normal D. A uniform plane wave has transverse E and H. Polarization describes the trajectory of E. Wave impedance relates E to H, and E×H is instantaneous power-flow density.','A free-space traveling wave has intrinsic impedance about 377 Ω. For a sinusoid using peak fields, average power density is |Epeak|²/(2η) in a lossless medium.','Which vector gives instantaneous electromagnetic power-flow density?','E × H',['E + H','E · H','H − E'],'The cross product is the Poynting vector.'),
t('Propagation in dielectrics and conductors, skin depth and interfaces','In a lossless medium, speed is 1/√με and wavelength is speed divided by frequency. In conducting or lossy media, fields attenuate. Skin depth is the distance over which field amplitude falls by e⁻¹, approximately √[2/(ωμσ)] in a good conductor. Impedance differences at an interface produce reflection and transmission.','Increasing frequency by a factor of four halves skin depth in the good-conductor approximation.','How far must a field travel into a conductor to fall to about 37% of its surface amplitude?','One skin depth',['One wavelength in all cases','Two skin depths','One quarter wavelength in all cases'],'Amplitude decays exponentially and e⁻¹ is approximately 0.368.'),
t('Line parameters, characteristic impedance and input impedance','Transmission lines distribute resistance, inductance, conductance and capacitance per unit length. The propagation constant γ=α+jβ describes attenuation and phase. For a lossless line, Z₀=√(L′/C′). Input impedance depends on load and electrical length. A matched load presents Z₀ at the input for any length.','One half-wavelength of lossless line repeats the load impedance at its input.','What is the input impedance of a lossless 50 Ω line terminated in 50 Ω?','50 Ω for any length',['0 Ω','Always twice the load','It alternates between open and short'],'A matched termination produces no reflection.'),
t('Reflection, VSWR, return loss and matching','For a lossless line with real Z₀, Γ=(ZL−Z₀)/(ZL+Z₀). The reflected power fraction is |Γ|². VSWR=(1+|Γ|)/(1−|Γ|), and return loss is −20 log₁₀|Γ|. Matching aims to reduce reflection. A matched line has Γ=0, VSWR=1 and ideal return loss tending to infinity.','A 100 Ω load on a 50 Ω line gives |Γ|=1/3, VSWR=2 and about 11.1% reflected power.','If |Γ| is 0.5, how much incident power is reflected?','25%',['50%','75%','100%'],'Reflection coefficient is a voltage-wave ratio, so square its magnitude for power.'),
t('Open and short lines, quarter-wave transformation, Smith chart and stubs','An ideal open reflects voltage with Γ=+1; a short gives Γ=−1. A lossless quarter-wave line has Zin=Zt²/ZL, so real resistances can be matched by Zt=√(Z₀RL). A Smith chart maps normalized impedance and reflection coefficient. A shunt stub adds susceptance to cancel the unwanted imaginary part at a chosen location.','Matching 100 Ω to 50 Ω with a quarter-wave transformer at the design frequency requires Zt≈70.7 Ω.','What impedance does an ideal short look like through a lossless quarter-wave line?','An open circuit',['A short circuit','The characteristic impedance','A fixed 50 Ω resistor'],'Quarter-wave impedance inversion turns zero load impedance into infinite input impedance ideally.'),
t('Waveguides, TE/TM modes, cutoff and cavity resonators','TE has no longitudinal electric field and TM has no longitudinal magnetic field. Each mode in a hollow guide has a cutoff, below which it is evanescent. The usual dominant mode of a rectangular guide with broad wall a is TE₁₀, with air-filled cutoff c/(2a). Cavity resonators enclose fields and support discrete resonances set by geometry and materials.','An air-filled guide with a=30 mm has TE₁₀ cutoff near 5 GHz.','What is the usual dominant mode of a rectangular hollow waveguide with unequal sides?','TE₁₀',['TM₁₀','TEM','TE₀₀'],'It has the lowest cutoff for broad-side dimension a greater than b.'),
t('S-parameters, dB, dBm, insertion loss and RF blocks','S-parameters relate incident and outgoing waves at ports with reference impedances. S11 is input reflection and S21 is forward transmission with other ports matched. dB expresses a ratio; dBm is power relative to 1 mW. A filter selects bands, an amplifier increases signal level, and a mixer translates frequency using a local oscillator. Insertion loss is often reported as positive for a passive network.','With matched ports, |S21|=0.5 corresponds to roughly −6 dB forward transmission or +6 dB insertion loss.','What does S11 describe?','Input reflection with other ports matched',['Forward transmission','Absolute input power in dBm','Output reflection only'],'The first index is the outgoing port and the second is the excited input port.')
],[['S11','Input reflection'],['VSWR of 1','Matched line'],['Quarter-wave transformer','Impedance inversion at design frequency'],['Poynting vector','Electromagnetic power-flow density']]),

s('antennas','Antennas, Propagation, and Radar',[
t('Dipoles, monopoles, radiation patterns and beamwidth','A dipole has two arms. A quarter-wave monopole over a conducting ground plane uses its image as the other half. A far-field radiation pattern shows radiation versus direction. Half-power beamwidth spans the −3 dB points around the main lobe. Sidelobes are smaller peaks and front-to-back ratio compares forward and reverse radiation.','A half-wave dipole radiates most strongly broadside and has nulls along its axis.','Where does an ideal straight half-wave dipole have radiation nulls?','Along its axis',['All broadside directions','Only at 45°','It has no nulls'],'Current along the wire produces no far-field radiation in the axial directions.'),
t('Radiation resistance, directivity, gain, efficiency and aperture','Radiation resistance represents power carried away as radiation relative to feed current. Directivity compares intensity in a direction to the average radiated intensity. Gain includes radiation efficiency, G=ηD. Effective aperture relates incident power density to received available power; Ae=λ²G/(4π) for matched polarization. Linear polarization mismatch contributes cos²θ for angular difference θ.','If the two linear polarizations differ by 60°, the power factor is cos²60°=0.25.','An antenna has directivity 4 (linear) and radiation efficiency 50%. What is gain?','2 (linear)',['4 (linear)','8 (linear)','0.5 (linear)'],'Multiply directivity by radiation efficiency.'),
t('Antenna arrays, array factor and beam steering','An array adds the fields from multiple elements. The array factor describes the contribution of element spacing and complex weights, combined with the individual element pattern under the usual identical-element model. A progressive phase shift moves the main beam. Spacing and steering determine whether unwanted grating lobes appear.','A uniform broadside linear array uses equal excitation phase. A phase progression changes the direction where fields add most strongly.','What is adjusted for electronic beam steering in a phased array?','Relative element phases',['The speed of light','Only the output display','The free-space permittivity'],'A controlled phase progression changes where the emitted fields reinforce.'),
t('Ground wave, sky wave, line of sight and propagation mechanisms','Ground waves follow the surface and tend to be more useful at lower frequencies. Sky waves can return toward Earth via ionospheric refraction under suitable conditions. Line-of-sight links use a direct path. Reflection redirects waves at interfaces, diffraction bends or spreads them around edges, and scattering redistributes energy from rough surfaces or small objects.','A ridge can reduce the direct path but still allow some energy into its shadow through diffraction.','Which mechanism is associated with propagation around an edge into a shadow?','Diffraction',['Only specular reflection','Refraction in the ionosphere only','Ohmic conduction'],'Edges produce diffracted fields even where simple geometric optics predicts no direct path.'),
t('Friis equation, free-space path loss and link budget','In matched far-field free-space conditions, Pr=PtGtGr[λ/(4πd)]² using linear gains. Free-space path loss is the reciprocal of the bracketed squared factor. In a dB link budget, add transmit power in dBm and both antenna gains, then subtract propagation and other losses in dB.','Doubling the free-space separation at fixed frequency reduces receive power by about 6 dB.','Which change causes a 6 dB increase in free-space path loss, other terms fixed?','Doubling the distance',['Halving the distance','Keeping distance fixed and halving frequency','Doubling transmit power'],'Free-space path loss grows with the square of distance.'),
t('Radar blocks, pulse and CW radar, echo delay and Doppler','A basic radar has a signal source/transmitter, antenna, receiver and signal processor. A simple pulse radar estimates range from two-way travel time, R=cΔt/2. An unmodulated CW radar can measure radial velocity with Doppler but gives no ordinary absolute range measurement by delay. For monostatic sensing, Doppler magnitude is 2|vr|/λ.','A 2 µs round-trip echo corresponds to about 300 m in air.','Why is echo delay multiplied by c/2 to find range?','The echo travels to the object and back',['Radio travels at half the speed of light','One pulse contains two carrier frequencies','A delayed echo has half the transmit power'],'The measured delay is for a path of length twice the one-way range.'),
t('Pulse width, PRF, PRI, range resolution and radar equation','PRI is the time between pulses and PRF=1/PRI. The simple unambiguous-range limit is c/(2PRF) for regularly spaced pulses. For an uncompressed rectangular pulse, range resolution is roughly cτ/2; more generally bandwidth B gives roughly c/(2B). The ideal monostatic radar equation has received echo power proportional to the fourth inverse power of range because energy spreads on the outward and return paths.','An uncompressed 1 µs pulse corresponds to roughly 150 m range resolution.','What happens to simple maximum unambiguous range when PRF doubles?','It halves',['It doubles','It stays fixed','It quadruples'],'The interval allowed before the next pulse is halved.')
],[['Beamwidth','Angle between main-lobe half-power points'],['Polarization mismatch','Power factor from relative polarization'],['Pulse radar range','Computed from round-trip echo delay'],['PRF','Number of pulses transmitted per second']]),

s('embedded','Microprocessors, Microcontrollers, and Embedded Systems',[
t('CPU, ALU, registers, memory and the instruction cycle','The CPU interprets program instructions. Its ALU performs arithmetic and logic, registers hold fast working values, and memory stores code and data. Address, data and control signals communicate across buses. In a basic instruction cycle, the program counter identifies the instruction to fetch, the decoder interprets it, and the execution stage performs the operation.','Fetching an instruction reads memory at the address held by the program counter, which then advances according to the instruction format or control transfer.','Which register identifies the next instruction to fetch in a basic CPU model?','Program counter',['Stack pointer','Data accumulator only','Status register'],'The program counter tracks instruction addresses.'),
t('Instruction sets, addressing modes, stack, subroutines and interrupts','An ISA defines instructions and their architectural effects. Immediate addressing stores the operand in the instruction. Register, direct, register-indirect and indexed forms obtain it from different locations or calculated addresses. A stack uses last-in, first-out ordering. A subroutine saves a return address; an interrupt redirects execution after saving enough state to resume.','A CALL commonly places the return address on a stack. RET uses it to resume the caller.','In immediate addressing, where is the operand encoded?','In the instruction itself',['Only in external I/O','Always at the stack top','At a separate memory address in every case'],'The immediate field directly represents the value used by the instruction.'),
t('8085, 8086, 8051 and RISC versus CISC','The 8085 is an 8-bit microprocessor with a 16-bit address bus. The 8086 has a 16-bit data path and uses segment plus offset addressing for its 20-bit address space. The 8051 is an 8-bit microcontroller family with on-chip peripheral functions. RISC often emphasizes regular encodings and load/store operations; CISC typically supports richer instruction forms.','An 8085 with 16 address bits can select 2¹⁶ byte locations, or 64 KiB.','Which classic device is a microcontroller with on-chip peripheral functions such as timers and I/O ports?','8051',['8085','8086','8087'],'The 8051 integrates a processor core with microcontroller peripherals.'),
t('Memory mapping, memory-mapped I/O and isolated I/O','An address map assigns ranges to memory and devices using address decoding. Memory-mapped I/O gives device registers ordinary memory addresses and uses normal load/store operations. Isolated I/O uses a distinct port-address space and typically dedicated access instructions. Device registers can have read or write side effects.','Writing a GPIO output register at a documented memory address can change external pin levels.','Which I/O scheme uses the same address space as memory?','Memory-mapped I/O',['Isolated I/O','DMA in all cases','Serial I/O only'],'Device registers appear in the ordinary memory address map.'),
t('Timers, counters, GPIO, PWM and peripheral interfacing','A timer counts internal clock ticks, often with a prescaler. A counter can count external events. GPIO pins can read or drive digital levels when configured correctly. PWM changes the fraction of a cycle spent high, with duty cycle Ton/T. Interfacing requires compatible voltage, drive, direction and timing.','At a 1 ms PWM period, a 250 µs high pulse gives 25% duty cycle.','What duty cycle results from a 2 ms high interval in an 8 ms period?','25%',['16%','50%','75%'],'Duty cycle equals high time divided by total period.'),
t('UART, SPI, I²C, polling, interrupts and DMA','UART uses asynchronous start/stop framing and an agreed baud rate. SPI uses a shared clock, data signals and device select. I²C uses addressed devices on two shared open-drain lines with pull-ups. Polling checks status repeatedly, interrupts notify the CPU of events, and DMA transfers blocks with limited CPU involvement.','An I²C sensor and EEPROM can share SDA and SCL if their addresses differ and the bus meets electrical limits.','Which interface normally uses addressed devices and two shared open-drain signal lines?','I²C',['UART','SPI','Parallel GPIO'],'I²C uses SDA and SCL with pull-ups and device addressing.')
],[['Program counter','Tracks instruction addresses'],['UART','Asynchronous framed serial link'],['DMA','Transfers data blocks with limited CPU intervention'],['Memory-mapped I/O','Uses addresses in the memory space']]),

s('measure','Measurements and Instrumentation',[
t('Accuracy, precision, resolution, sensitivity and loading','Accuracy is closeness to the reference value and precision is repeatability. Resolution is the smallest displayed or detectable step. Sensitivity is output change per input change. A meter can disturb the circuit through finite input impedance. Measurement error compares the indication with a reference, and uncertainty describes the range of plausible values.','A voltmeter with input resistance comparable to the node source resistance can read low because it forms a divider.','Which term describes closely repeated readings even if they are offset from the true value?','Precision',['Accuracy','Sensitivity','Loading'],'Precision concerns repeatability, so a systematic bias can coexist with high precision.'),
t('Multimeters, oscilloscopes, generators and spectrum analyzers','A multimeter measures electrical quantities, with voltage measured in parallel and current in series through a suitable input. A CRO or DSO displays voltage over time; a DSO digitizes and can store records. A function generator provides known stimuli. A spectrum analyzer gives frequency-domain levels and can show harmonics and spurs.','To inspect pulse rise time, use a suitably fast oscilloscope. To inspect harmonic frequencies directly, use a spectrum analyzer.','Which instrument directly displays the amplitudes of spectral components versus frequency?','Spectrum analyzer',['Ordinary DC ammeter','Function generator','Wheatstone bridge'],'A spectrum analyzer resolves signal power or amplitude across frequency.'),
t('Oscilloscope amplitude, period, frequency and phase','Multiply vertical divisions by volts/division and the probe factor to find voltage. Multiply horizontal divisions by time/division to get elapsed time. Frequency is reciprocal period. For equal-frequency sinusoids, phase displacement magnitude is 360°×Δt/T, with sign determined by which waveform leads.','If one waveform crosses zero 0.5 ms before the other and their period is 2 ms, it leads by 90°.','A trace spans four horizontal divisions per cycle at 0.5 ms/div. What is frequency?','500 Hz',['250 Hz','2 kHz','50 Hz'],'The period is 2 ms, so the frequency is 1/0.002 Hz.'),
t('Wheatstone bridge and AC bridges','A Wheatstone bridge compares resistive divider voltages. The detector is zero when the arm ratios match. AC bridges extend balancing to complex impedances, so the magnitude and phase balance together. Different bridge topologies are chosen for resistance, capacitance or inductance and loss measurements.','When the left arm divider ratio is 1 kΩ/2 kΩ, a right ratio of 2 kΩ/4 kΩ produces balance in the corresponding connection.','At Wheatstone bridge balance, what does the detector measure?','Zero differential voltage',['Maximum branch voltage','Infinite resistor current','An AC phase shift of 90° automatically'],'The two divider midpoints have equal voltage.'),
t('Strain gauges, RTDs, thermistors, thermocouples and LVDTs','A strain gauge converts deformation into resistance change and is often used in a bridge. An RTD uses the resistance change of a metal with temperature. A thermistor uses a strongly temperature-dependent semiconductor resistance. A thermocouple generates a voltage from a temperature difference. An LVDT uses differential transformer coupling to sense linear displacement.','For a strain gauge, fractional resistance change equals gauge factor × strain in the linear range.','Which transducer uses differential transformer coupling to measure linear displacement?','LVDT',['Thermocouple','RTD','Strain gauge'],'Motion of its core changes the relative coupling to the two secondary windings.'),
t('Signal conditioning, instrumentation gain, grounding and shielding','Signal conditioning can amplify weak sensor signals, filter noise, level-shift or isolate signals and adapt them to converters. An instrumentation amplifier amplifies a small differential voltage amid common-mode interference while presenting high input impedance. Grounding establishes references and return paths. Shielding limits coupling, and its termination must suit the interference type and frequency.','A bridge sensor may deliver a few millivolts on a much larger common-mode level. A high-CMRR instrumentation amplifier amplifies the difference.','Which amplifier property helps reject pickup present equally on both sensor leads?','High CMRR',['Low input impedance','Large offset voltage','High output ripple'],'Common-mode rejection suppresses voltage shared by both inputs.')
],[['Accuracy','Closeness to the reference value'],['Sensitivity','Change in output per change in input'],['LVDT','Linear displacement sensor'],['Wheatstone bridge','Resistive ratio measurement at balance']])
];
